
{"id":"de42374e-3be8-4afb-a2d3-1fea1fb47027","object":"chat.completion","created":1785032221,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that returns an object with fields: attempts (integer), one_minimal (boolean), reduced_items (list of strings). The input case payload has \"items\" (list of strings) and \"required_failure_items\" (list of strings). The task is about chunk reduction: start with granularity 2, try chunks left to right, attempt to remove a chunk (i.e., produce a reduced list where that chunk is omitted) and check if required_failure_items remain (i.e., all required_failure_items are still present in the reduced list). If removal is accepted, we restart with slightly coarser granularity? Wait: \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" That is ambiguous. Let's interpret: start granularity 2. For each granularity, we try to remove chunks (contiguous blocks of that size) from left to right. If removal of a chunk results in all required_failure_items still present, we accept that removal, then restart from the beginning with granularity increased by 1? Or \"slightly coarser\" means increase granularity? Actually \"coarser\" means larger chunk size? Typically in delta debugging, granularity is the chunk size; smaller granularity is finer. Here \"start chunk removal at granularity two\" means chunk size 2. \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity\" suggests after a successful removal, we increase the granularity (make chunks larger). \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity\" means when no removal succeeds at current granularity, we double the granularity; stop when granularity becomes 1? Actually singleton granularity might be 1. But if we double from 2 -> 4 -> 8... until > len(items) maybe stop. But they say \"stop at singleton granularity\" which is 1. That seems contradictory with doubling. Let's read carefully: \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\" That could mean: if no removal succeeds at current granularity, then double granularity; if after doubling we get a granularity that is more than half the length? Actually \"singleton granularity\" might mean granularity of 1. But doubling from 2 gives 4, 8, etc. To reach 1, we would need to divide. Maybe they mean \"stop when granularity becomes 1\"? But they say \"double\" which is increase. Let's re-read: \"Start chunk removal at granularity two. Try chunks from left to right and accept removal only if required failure items remain. Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity. Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\" This is ambiguous. Possibly \"coarser\" means smaller? No, coarser means larger. In delta debugging, typically you start with large chunks and refine to smaller. Here they start with granularity 2 (which is small) and after success they go coarser (larger), and when no success they double (make even coarser) and stop when granularity becomes 1? That doesn't align. Maybe it's a typo: \"coarser\" might be \"finer\"? Let's examine typical chunk reduction: You start with a list, you try to remove large chunks, if removal doesn't preserve failure, you go to smaller chunks. But here they say \"start at granularity two\" (small), \"restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity\" (larger), \"double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity\" (smaller). That is weird.\n\nAlternatively, it could be interpreted as: We are trying to reduce the list by removing chunks, but we want to keep required failure items. So we start with chunk size 2 (small chunks). We try to remove each chunk (starting from left, contiguous block of size 2). If removal of a chunk keeps all required items, then we accept that removal, and then we restart the process from the beginning but with a slightly coarser granularity (i.e., larger chunk size, e.g., increase by 1). If no removal succeeds at current granularity, then we double the granularity (make even larger) until we reach granularity that is larger than the length? But they say \"stop at singleton granularity\" which is 1. That suggests that doubling would eventually make granularity large, not small. Unless \"singleton granularity\" means granularity such that chunks are of size 1? That's fine.\n\nMaybe \"singleton granularity\" means that the process stops when the granularity becomes 1 (i.e., chunks are single items). But then doubling from 2 -> 4 -> 8 ... never reaches 1. So maybe they mean \"divide granularity by 2\"? Or \"stop at the point where granularity is 1 (i.e., smallest possible)\". But they say \"double granularity when no removal succeeds\". That would increase granularity, not decrease. If we start at 2 and no removal succeeds, doubling gives 4; if still no success, double to 8, etc. That would make granularity larger, and we stop at \"singleton granularity\" which is 1? That makes no sense.\n\nLet's examine the public fixtures. They have 10 items. Required_failure_items: [t0, t6] for first case. What is the expected reduced_items? We don't have expected outputs. But we can infer from the task description: \"Return the reduced items, attempt count, and an explicit one-minimal check.\" The one_minimal boolean likely indicates whether the reduced items are minimal (i.e., cannot be further reduced while preserving required failure items). So we need to simulate the algorithm.\n\nLet's re-express the algorithm as per common delta debugging: \n- Start with chunk size = 2.\n- For each chunk (contiguous block of size chunk_size) from left to right, try to remove it. If after removal, all required_failure_items are still present, then accept that removal, and then restart the process from the beginning with chunk_size = 2 again? Or with slightly coarser? The instruction says \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" That suggests after a successful removal, we increase chunk_size by some small amount (maybe +1). \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\" means if we try all chunks at current chunk_size and none works, then we double chunk_size. \"Stop at singleton granularity\" means we stop when chunk_size becomes 1? But doubling never yields 1. Perhaps \"singleton granularity\" means when chunk_size equals the length of the current list? That is, if the list has length L, then singleton granularity might be L? Unclear.\n\nLet's look up typical \"chunk reduction\" or \"delta debugging\" algorithm: In the classic ddmin algorithm, you start with chunk size = len/2, then try to remove each chunk. If a removal succeeds, you continue with that reduced list and the same chunk size. If no removal succeeds, you halve the chunk size. You stop when chunk size is 1. That is the typical \"bisecting\" approach. Here they mention \"double granularity\" but the classic is halve. They say \"double granularity when no removal succeeds\" means increase granularity (make chunks larger). That is opposite of typical. But note: they start at granularity 2, which is already small. If no removal succeeds, they double granularity (make larger chunks). If a removal succeeds, they restart with slightly coarser (larger). So the trend is to move to larger chunks. That might be a different algorithm: starting from small chunks, if we can remove small chunks successfully, we then try larger chunks to see if we can remove even more. And if no small chunk removal works, we try larger chunks. The stop condition is \"singleton granularity\" which might mean when chunk size equals the length of the list? That would be the largest chunk (singleton as in one chunk covering entire list). But then they'd stop. That could be plausible.\n\nLet's test with first case: items = [t0,t1,t2,t3,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9], required = [t0,t6]. Start granularity 2. Chunks: [t0,t1], [t2,t3], [t4,t5], [t6,t7], [t8,t9]. Try from left: remove [t0,t1] -> remaining [t2,t3,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9] still has t0? t0 is removed, so it fails. Next chunk [t2,t3] removal -> remaining [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9] still has t0 and t6? t0 and t6 present, so accept. Reduced list becomes [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9]. Then restart with slightly coarser granularity. What is \"slightly coarser\"? Possibly increase by 1 -> granularity 3. Now list length 8. Chunks of size 3: [t0,t1,t4], [t5,t6,t7], [t8] (since last chunk may be shorter). Try remove first chunk -> remaining [t5,t6,t7,t8] has t0? No t0 removed. So fail. Next chunk [t5,t6,t7] removal -> remaining [t0,t1,t4,t8] has t6? t6 removed, fail. Next chunk [t8] removal -> remaining [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7] has t6? t6 present, t0 present, accept. Reduced list becomes [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7]. Now restart with coarser maybe granularity 4? Then try chunks of size 4: [t0,t1,t4,t5], [t6,t7]. Remove first -> remaining [t6,t7] has t0? no. Fail. Remove second -> remaining [t0,t1,t4,t5] has t6? no. Fail. So no success. Then \"double granularity when no removal succeeds\": granularity becomes 8 (double from 4). But list length is 6, so only one chunk of size 6 (if we allow chunk size to be larger than list? Maybe we cap at list length). Remove the only chunk (all items) -> remaining empty -> t0 and t6 missing -> fail. Then double granularity to 16, stop? But \"stop at singleton granularity\" maybe means stop when granularity equals 1? Not matching.\n\nAlternatively, maybe \"singleton granularity\" means when the granularity becomes larger than the list length, i.e., no chunks can be formed, stop. That is more plausible.\n\nBut the algorithm as described is unusual. We need to implement something that will produce deterministic results for the given fixtures. Since we don't have expected outputs, we need to implement a reasonable interpretation that the evaluator will expect. The task says \"Apply deterministic chunk-removal and granularity-refinement passes while preserving a supplied syntax and interestingness oracle.\" The oracle is that required failure items must remain. The syntax is the item strings.\n\nLet's look at the fixture payload: They include \"_case_nonce\" but that's likely irrelevant. The payload has \"items\" and \"required_failure_items\". That's all.\n\nI think the most standard interpretation is the classic ddmin algorithm (delta debugging) for minimization. In ddmin, you start with granularity = 2 (or half the length), but here they explicitly say start at 2. So we'll assume granularity = 2. Then: For each chunk (contiguous block of current granularity), try removal. If removal preserves all required items (i.e., all required_failure_items are in the remaining list), then accept that removal and continue with the same granularity on the new list (restart from beginning of list). This is typical: after a successful removal, you keep the same granularity and try again from the start. If no removal succeeds, you halve the granularity (make finer) and continue. Stop when granularity is 1 (singleton). But here they say \"double granularity when no removal succeeds\" which is the opposite. Could be a misstatement or they meant \"halve\". Let's check the text: \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" That might mean after acceptance, you increase granularity (coarser) to try to remove larger chunks. \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\" might mean after trying all granularities from current up to some max and none succeed? Or it could be a two-phase process: first try small chunks, then increase.\n\nAnother possibility: The algorithm is like \"greedy chunk removal with increasing chunk size\". Start with granularity 2, try all chunks. If any removal succeeds, you restart from beginning with granularity+1 (or slightly coarser). If no removal succeeds, double granularity. Continue until granularity > length/2? That might converge.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I need to decide on a deterministic algorithm that is plausible and consistent with the name \"chunk_reduction_schedule\". The output must have attempts (count of removal attempts), one_minimal (whether the final reduced list is minimal, i.e., no further removal possible), and reduced_items.\n\nLet's implement the classic ddmin algorithm but with starting granularity 2 and halving on failure, stopping at granularity 1. That is a standard delta debugging. The \"one_minimal\" check would be true if after the algorithm, no single element can be removed while preserving required items (i.e., each element is necessary). That is a typical minimality check. The attempts would count the number of times we test a removal.\n\nI'll go with that. However, the instruction says \"Try chunks from left to right and accept removal only if required failure items remain. Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" Classic ddmin after success does not change granularity; it keeps same granularity. But they say \"slightly coarser\". That might mean after acceptance, we increase the granularity by 1 (or to the next integer). But then that would be like: after a successful removal of a chunk of size g, we then try larger chunks to see if we can remove even more. That also is plausible. Let's design an algorithm:\n\nInitialize list = items, g = 2, attempts = 0.\nLoop:\n - If g > len(list): break? Or stop at singleton granularity (when g == len(list)?)\n - For each chunk start index i from 0 to len(list)-g step g? Or sliding? They say \"Try chunks from left to right\". I'll assume contiguous non-overlapping chunks of size g. For each such chunk, construct a new list by removing that chunk, check if all required_failure_items are in new list. If yes, then accept: set list = new list, attempts++ (count each trial as an attempt), then restart from beginning with g = g + 1? (slightly coarser). If no, continue.\n - After all chunks tried with no success, then double g (g = g*2). If g > len(list), set g = len(list) maybe? Or stop? They say stop at singleton granularity. Singleton could mean g = 1? But doubling will not give 1. So maybe \"singleton granularity\" means g = len(list) (i.e., one big chunk). And we stop when g > len(list)? That would imply that after doubling, if g exceeds len(list), we stop.\n\nBut we also need to ensure that we eventually stop. The algorithm will keep increasing g, so eventually g > len(list) and we stop. The \"one_minimal\" check: after algorithm ends, we can test if any single element (g=1) can be removed. But not required by algorithm? The output wants one_minimal boolean. It might be whether the final list is minimal with respect to single-element removal. So after the loop, we can check if any single item can be removed while keeping required items. If none, one_minimal = True.\n\nLet's test with first case using this interpretation:\n\nInitial list L = [t0,t1,t2,t3,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9], required = {t0,t6}, g=2.\nChunks: [0:2], [2:4], [4:6], [6:8], [8:10]. Attempt each (attempts count trials). \n- remove chunk0: new list [t2,t3,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9] missing t0 -> fail.\n- remove chunk1: new [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9] has both -> success. Update L, g=3 (coarser). Reset i to 0.\nNow L = [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9] length 8, g=3.\nChunks of size 3: [0:3], [3:6], [6:8] (last chunk may be smaller if not divisible). Actually if g=3, the chunks are [0:3] (t0,t1,t4), [3:6] (t5,t6,t7), [6:8] (t8,t9). Attempt:\n- remove first: new [t5,t6,t7,t8,t9] missing t0 -> fail.\n- remove second: new [t0,t1,t4,t8,t9] missing t6 -> fail.\n- remove third: new [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7] has both -> success. L = [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7], g=4 (coarser), reset.\nNow L length 6, g=4.\nChunks: [0:4], [4:6] (since 6-4=2, last chunk size 2). Try:\n- remove first: new [t4,t5,t6,t7]? Actually remove [0:4] gives [t4,t5,t6,t7]? Wait L = [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7], indices 0..5. Remove [0:4] -> indices 4-5 => [t6,t7]? No, careful: L = [t0, t1, t4, t5, t6, t7]. Remove indices 0-3 gives [t6,t7] (since remaining indices 4,5). That list: [t6,t7] missing t0 -> fail.\n- remove second chunk [4:6] (indices 4,5) -> remove t6,t7 -> new [t0,t1,t4,t5] missing t6 -> fail.\nNo success. So double g: g=8. g > len(L)=6? 8 > 6, so maybe we stop or set g=len(L)? If we set g=6, then chunk is entire list. Remove it gives empty -> missing both -> fail. Then double to 12 > len, stop. At this point attempts count: we made attempts for each trial: for g=2: 2 attempts? Actually we attempted 2 chunks (first fail, second success) so 2 attempts for g=2. For g=3: we attempted 3 chunks (first fail, second fail, third success) -> 3 attempts. For g=4: 2 attempts (two chunks, both fail). Then for g=8 (or g=6): 1 attempt. Total attempts = 2+3+2+1 = 8. Reduced list = [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7]. Now we need to compute one_minimal: check if any single element can be removed while preserving required. Test removing each element (attempts extra? Maybe one_minimal is separate). Remove t0 -> lose t0 -> fail. Remove t1 -> list still has t0,t6? Yes, t0 and t6 remain. So removal of t1 is acceptable. So one_minimal should be False. So final output: attempts=8, one_minimal=False, reduced_items=[t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7].\n\nBut is that correct? Let's see if there is a smaller reduced list? Possibly we could remove t1 and t4? But algorithm might not find that. However the task is to implement the described algorithm exactly, not to find absolute minimal. So the output depends on the algorithm.\n\nNow check second fixture: required = [t0,t7]. Let's simulate quickly. Items same. g=2, chunks: [0:2] remove -> missing t0 -> fail. [2:4] remove -> new [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9] has both -> success. L = [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9], g=3. Chunks: [0:3] remove -> [t5,t6,t7,t8,t9] missing t0 -> fail. [3:6] remove -> [t0,t1,t4,t7,t8,t9]? Actually indices 3-5 are t5,t6,t7? Wait careful: L = [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9]. Chunks: [0:3] = t0,t1,t4; [3:6] = t5,t6,t7; [6:8] = t8,t9. Remove [3:6] gives [t0,t1,t4,t8,t9] missing t7? t7 is removed -> fail. Remove [6:8] gives [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7] has both -> success. L = [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7], g=4. Chunks: [0:4] = t0,t1,t4,t5; [4:6] = t6,t7. Remove [0:4] -> [t6,t7] missing t0 -> fail. Remove [4:6] -> [t0,t1,t4,t5] missing t7 -> fail. Double g=8. g>6, maybe try g=6: remove all -> fail. Stop. Reduced list = [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7]. Check one_minimal: remove t1? still has t0,t7? Yes, so False. This looks similar.\n\nThird fixture: required = [t1,t5]. Simulate. g=2, chunks: [0:2] remove -> new [t2,t3,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9] missing t1 -> fail. [2:4] remove -> [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9] has both? t1 and t5 present? Yes. So success. L = [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9], g=3. Chunks: [0:3] = t0,t1,t4; remove -> [t5,t6,t7,t8,t9] missing t1 -> fail. [3:6] = t5,t6,t7; remove -> [t0,t1,t4,t8,t9] missing t5 -> fail. [6:8] = t8,t9; remove -> [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7] has both -> success. L = [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7], g=4. Chunks: [0:4], [4:6]. Remove [0:4] -> [t6,t7] missing t1 and t5 -> fail. Remove [4:6] -> [t0,t1,t4,t5] missing t6? but required t1,t5 present? Actually remove [4:6] = t6,t7, new list = [t0,t1,t4,t5] has t1 and t5 -> success! So we accept removal of chunk [4:6] (t6,t7). Then L becomes [t0,t1,t4,t5], g=5 (coarser). g=5 > len=4? So maybe we stop or set g=len? If we set g=4 (since len=4) then chunk is entire list, remove -> empty -> fail. Then double to 8, stop. Final reduced list = [t0,t1,t4,t5]. Check one_minimal: remove t0? still has t1,t5 -> accept? Actually remove t0: list [t1,t4,t5] has t1 and t5 -> yes, so not minimal. So one_minimal False. Attempts: g=2: 2 attempts (first fail, second success)=2; g=3: 3 attempts (fail,fail,success)=3; g=4: 2 attempts (first fail, second success)=2; then g=5: maybe 1 attempt for entire list if g=len=4? Actually after success at g=4, we set g=5. Now L=[t0,t1,t4,t5] len=4. g=5>len, so maybe we treat g=len? If we follow rule \"stop at singleton granularity\", maybe singleton means g=len? That would be 4. Then try removing entire list -> fail. Then double g=8>len stop. So total attempts = 2+3+2+1=8. Reduced list = [t0,t1,t4,t5].\n\nNow we see that the algorithm produces plausible reduced lists. The one_minimal check for third case: removing t0 works, so one_minimal=False.\n\nNow, we need to count attempts correctly. Each time we try a chunk removal, that's an attempt. The algorithm as described should count the number of trials. Also note: we should not mutate case; return new list.\n\nNow we need to implement solve(case). The function receives case which is the payload object (dictionary with keys items, required_failure_items). It should return a dict with keys attempts, one_minimal, reduced_items.\n\nWe'll implement as described. We'll need to be careful with the logic for granularity progression.\n\nLet's outline:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    items = case['items'][:]  # copy\n    required = set(case['required_failure_items'])\n    g = 2\n    attempts = 0\n    # We'll keep a loop that modifies items in place\n    while True:\n        if g > len(items):\n            # stop? According to \"stop at singleton granularity\" might mean when g > len(items) break.\n            # Or maybe when g == len(items) we still try, then stop.\n            # Let's define: if g > len(items): break\n            break\n        # For non-overlapping chunks of size g (but last chunk may be smaller if not divisible? The description says \"granularity\" often means chunk size, and we try contiguous blocks of that exact size? Typically in delta debugging, you split into n chunks of roughly equal size. But here they say \"Try chunks from left to right\". I think they mean sliding windows? Often chunk reduction uses non-overlapping contiguous blocks of size g. But they say \"granularity two\" means split into pairs. So I'll use non-overlapping.\n        # Compute number of chunks: n = (len(items) + g - 1) // g\n        # But the last chunk may be smaller than g. Should we try that? Yes, it's a chunk.\n        # For each start index i in range(0, len(items), g):\n        i = 0\n        removed = False\n        while i < len(items):\n            chunk_end = min(i+g, len(items))\n            # Create candidate list without chunk i...i+g-1\n            candidate = items[:i] + items[chunk_end:]\n            attempts += 1\n            if required.issubset(candidate):\n                # Accept removal\n                items = candidate\n                # Restart with coarser granularity: g = g + 1?\n                # \"slightly coarser\" - we'll increment by 1.\n                g += 1\n                removed = True\n                break  # restart the loop from beginning\n            else:\n                i = chunk_end  # move to next chunk\n        if not removed:\n            # No removal succeeded at this granularity, double granularity\n            g *= 2\n            # Continue loop\n        # Optionally, if we break out of while True with break condition g > len(items) after doubling, we need to handle.\n        # Actually after doubling, we check condition at top of loop.\n    # After loop, we have items as final reduced list\n    # Now compute one_minimal: check if any single element removal preserves required\n    one_minimal = True\n    for idx in range(len(items)):\n        candidate = items[:idx] + items[idx+1:]\n        if required.issubset(candidate):\n            one_minimal = False\n            break\n    return {\n        \"attempts\": attempts,\n        \"one_minimal\": one_minimal,\n        \"reduced_items\": items\n    }\n\nBut we need to ensure that the loop terminates. With the above, if g exceeds len(items) after doubling, we break at top. Also after a successful removal, we increment g and restart the while loop with new items and new g. This should converge because items length decreases over time, and g increases, eventually g > len(items).\n\nNow test on third case